WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.220
People go to the movies to be entertained:
to laugh, to cry, to be shocked.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.469
But sometimes, moviegoers get way more than
they expected and end up running for the door.

00:00:09.469 --> 00:00:13.129
Here's a look at some movies that audiences
just couldn't take any more.

00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:14.759
A Clockwork Orange

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:19.890
Stanley Kubrick's adaptation of Anthony Burgess'
chilling novel was instantly considered brilliant,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.670
dangerous, and also a little maddening.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.990
Actor Malcolm McDowell said he saw people
"walking out of screenings in droves," with

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:29.230
many viewers outraged by the ultra-violent
content.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:33.680
Although Kubrick stood by the movie, a rash
of copycat crimes in England convinced the

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.780
studio to pull it from theaters there.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.700
Meanwhile, it became a smash in the U.S.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.360
After all, Americans do love to dance.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.280
"Singing in the rain.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:48.760
I'm singing, I'm singing in the rain."

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.449
Swiss Army Man

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:55.030
Everyone knows flatulence can clear a room,
but when Sundance Film Festival audiences

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.170
got a whiff of Swiss Army Man, it nearly emptied
the theater according to Variety.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.170
Why?

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.350
Well, Daniel Radcliffe plays a rotting corpse
that Paul Dano's character rides like a jet

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:08.460
ski — propelled only by Radcliffe's, uh,
natural gas.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.920
The movie wasn't a total stinker, though:
Critics have given it mostly favorable reviews.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.170
"Uh, that's weird, people don't like other
peoples farts."

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.720
The Blair Witch Project

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.880
While the low budget of The Blair Witch Project
brought an exceptional financial return to

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.100
Artisan Films, it did cost a lot of viewers
their lunches.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.920
The shaky, found footage-style camerawork
had people hitting the deck — and not necessarily

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:30.979
out of fear.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:35.380
Many audience members walked out due to nausea
and panic attacks, while others gave up due

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.680
to the movie's slow pace.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.229
"It's not the same log Mike."

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:40.630
Hey, there's nothing scarier than evil logs!

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.560
And not the poopy kind.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.909
"Please help us"

00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:46.090
The Exorcist

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:51.320
Warner Bros. marketed The Exorcist as "something
beyond comprehension" and, for a lot of theater-goers

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.210
in 1973, that was exactly right.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.549
Thanks to word of mouth, the film became a
must-see event, but once the frames started

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.549
rolling, many people couldn't handle what
they were seeing, such as the horrifying image

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:06.920
of a little girl twisting her head completely
around or, well, pretty much anything else

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.920
from the movie.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.020
Plenty of people were too grossed out or terrified
to finish the film, but others left because

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.040
they were offended by the religious implications.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.180
Hey, some religions just don't like pea soup.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.800
"Mmm, sacrilecious."

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.080
Reservoir Dogs

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.050
Quentin Tarantino's directorial debut established
him as a true talent, and the film's torture

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:34.220
scene still serves as a yardstick by which
the concept of "twisted" is measured.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.410
The now-iconic scene was too gory for some
people, though, and it prompted many early

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:39.410
exits.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:40.440
But Tarantino took it as a compliment.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.440
He said:

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.380
"It never bothered me when people walked out.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.910
It just meant the scene worked"

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:49.810
A little too well: famous horror make-up artist
Rick Baker left a screener during the scene,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:54.190
saying he was in the business of fantasy-based
scares, and Tarantino was "dealing with real-life

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.190
violence."

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:56.190
Pulp Fiction

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.850
Tarantino's second movie won him an Oscar
for best screenplay, but the film wasn't universally

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.220
beloved when it came out.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.390
The non-linear storytelling, graphic violence,
and rape scenes led to walk-outs, with Tarantino

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.980
famously being heckled during his acceptance
speech for the prestigious Palme d'Or at the

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.290
Cannes Film Festival.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.880
And those who walked out were luckier than
some others who had to be carried out.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.080
The scene in which John Travolta thrusts a
hypodermic needle into Uma Thurman's chest

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.050
caused multiple audience members to faint.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.250
The Lion King

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.280
The circle of life may sound beautiful in
song, but some kids and their parents didn't

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.770
think it was so fantastic when King Mufasa
was gruesomely murdered on screen by way of

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.140
a wildebeest stampede.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.260
"Great idea, who needs a king?"

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.840
"No king, no king, la la la la la."

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.470
Many shocked parents had to remove their horrified
children from the audience.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.550
"Pumba, not in front of the kids."

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.980
Guess it gives a whole new meaning to the
term "Scarred for life."

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.120
Am I right Lads, or am I right Lads, Cause
the bad guy is named Scar?

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.120
Get it?

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.120
Please anyone...

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.120
Thanks for watching!

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.140
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.930
And leave us a comment to let us know which
movies were just too much for you to take…

